\title{CalZSAD: Conformal Few-Normal-Shot Thresholding
for CLIP-based Zero-Shot Anomaly Detection with FPR Control}

\author{Nguyen~Duong,~250101084,
        Dinh~Thi~Thu~Hiep,~250101085,
        and~Nguyen~Minh~Chien,~250101080%
\thanks{University of Information Technology, Vietnam National University Ho Chi Minh City.}}

\markboth{Deep Learning -- Final Report, 2026}%
{Duong \MakeLowercase{\textit{et al.}}: CalZSAD for CLIP-based Zero-Shot Anomaly Detection}

\maketitle

% =====================================================================
% ABSTRACT
% =====================================================================
\begin{abstract}
Anomaly detection plays a critical role in industrial quality inspection and medical image analysis, where the goal is to identify samples that deviate from the normal distribution at both image and pixel levels. Recent CLIP-based zero-shot anomaly detection (CLIP-ZSAD) methods, notably AA-CLIP, achieve image-level AUROC above 90\% on standard benchmarks; however, they only produce continuous anomaly scores without a decision threshold, making direct deployment infeasible. Three obstacles hinder practical use: anomaly scores shift across object classes, pixel-AUROC is inflated by extreme class imbalance where over 99\% of pixels are normal, and thresholds calibrated on a source domain degrade when transferred to a new target domain. This work addresses the gap between benchmark performance and operational readiness by applying CalZSAD, a lightweight, model-agnostic post-processing module on top of the AA-CLIP backbone. CalZSAD combines robust per-class normalization using the median and median absolute deviation, an image-level signal derived from the mean of the top-1\% highest-scoring pixels, and conformal prediction-based threshold selection requiring only 20 normal images per class and no defective samples. Experiments on six datasets spanning industrial and medical domains show that CalZSAD maintains a false positive rate (FPR) of 0.94 to 5.74\% around the 5\% target while achieving F1 scores within 5 to 9 points of the oracle upper bound. At the pixel level, Dice at a false alarm rate of 1\% closely matches the oracle, confirming that the threshold selection mechanism performs near its theoretical limit.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Zero-shot anomaly detection, CLIP, AA-CLIP, conformal prediction, threshold selection, false positive rate control.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\IEEEpeerreviewmaketitle

% =====================================================================
% I. INTRODUCTION
% =====================================================================
\section{Introduction}

\IEEEPARstart{A}{nomaly} detection aims to identify samples that deviate from the normal distribution and is essential in domains ranging from industrial quality control~\cite{bergmann2019mvtec, mishra2021btad} to medical image screening~\cite{bao2023bmad}. In manufacturing, detecting surface defects, structural cracks, or assembly errors prevents costly recalls; in medicine, flagging abnormal regions in brain MRI, liver CT, or retinal scans supports early diagnosis. Because defective samples are inherently rare and diverse, supervised approaches that require large labeled defect datasets are impractical. Consequently, unsupervised and zero-shot methods that learn only from normal data have gained significant attention.

The advent of large-scale vision-language models, particularly CLIP~\cite{radford2021clip}, has enabled zero-shot anomaly detection (ZSAD) in which detectors identify defects on entirely unseen object classes without any class-specific training data. Several CLIP-ZSAD methods have been proposed with different adaptation strategies. WinCLIP~\cite{jeong2023winclip} employs handcrafted text prompts combined with multi-scale windowing and is entirely training-free. AnomalyCLIP~\cite{zhou2024anomalyclip} learns object-agnostic prompts independent of class names. AdaCLIP~\cite{cao2024adaclip} combines static and dynamic prompts generated per image. Most recently, AA-CLIP~\cite{ma2025aaclip} separates normal and abnormal semantics in the language space before aligning visual features to these anchors, achieving state-of-the-art AUROC on multiple benchmarks. AA-CLIP outperforms prior methods by explicitly constructing anomaly-aware anchors that sharpen the separation between normal and anomalous feature distributions, leading to higher ranking performance especially on challenging industrial and medical datasets. Despite these advances, all existing methods share a fundamental limitation: they produce continuous anomaly scores but provide \emph{no decision threshold}. In practice, whether on a production line or in a screening system, a binary decision (``normal'' or ``anomalous'') is required, and this decision depends critically on a well-chosen threshold.

This work addresses the deployment gap by applying CalZSAD, a lightweight post-processing module, on top of the frozen AA-CLIP backbone. CalZSAD consists of three components: (i)~replacing the native image-level score with the mean of the top-1\% highest-scoring pixels from the anomaly map, which better separates normal from anomalous images; (ii)~robust per-class normalization using the median and half-MAD to eliminate score scale shifts across classes; and (iii)~conformal prediction-based threshold selection using only $K{=}20$ normal images per class, which provides finite-sample guarantees on the false positive rate without requiring any defective samples or distributional assumptions. The entire pipeline is model-agnostic: it processes only the output scores of the frozen backbone and can be applied to any CLIP-ZSAD model.

Our main contributions are twofold. First, we propose CalZSAD as a practical post-processing framework that transforms raw anomaly scores from AA-CLIP into deployment-ready binary decisions with controlled FPR. Second, we conduct a comprehensive evaluation on six datasets across industrial and medical domains, reporting not only conventional ranking metrics (AUROC, AP, AUPRO, AUPIMO) but also operational metrics (F1, Recall, FPR, Dice) at a fixed operating point, thereby revealing the gap between high AUROC and actual deployability.

% =====================================================================
% II. RELATED WORK
% =====================================================================
\section{Related Work}

\subsection{CLIP-based Zero-Shot Anomaly Detection}

CLIP-based ZSAD methods leverage the vision-language alignment of CLIP~\cite{radford2021clip} to detect anomalies on unseen classes. WinCLIP~\cite{jeong2023winclip} is a training-free approach that uses handcrafted prompts describing normal and abnormal states, combined with multi-scale windowing to generate pixel-level anomaly maps. AnomalyCLIP~\cite{zhou2024anomalyclip} learns a set of object-agnostic prompts that generalize across different object categories without relying on class names. AdaCLIP~\cite{cao2024adaclip} extends this by combining static prompts with dynamic prompts generated adaptively for each input image. AA-CLIP~\cite{ma2025aaclip}, the backbone used in this work, introduces anomaly-aware anchors that explicitly separate normal and abnormal semantics in the language embedding space before aligning visual features to these anchors via residual adapters. This design yields stronger separation between normal and anomalous distributions compared to methods that rely on generic prompts, and AA-CLIP achieves state-of-the-art image-level AUROC on benchmarks including MVTec-AD and VisA. However, like all other methods in this family, AA-CLIP outputs a continuous anomaly score and does not address the question of where to set the decision boundary.

\subsection{Evaluation Beyond AUROC}

The standard metric for anomaly detection benchmarks is AUROC. However, at the pixel level, over 99\% of pixels are normal, which causes pixel-AUROC to appear optimistically high even when the anomaly map is imprecise~\cite{saito2015pr}. Average Precision (AP), which focuses on the positive (anomalous) class, provides a more faithful assessment under such imbalance. AUPRO~\cite{bergmann2020aupro} measures per-region overlap at a bounded false positive rate, and AUPIMO~\cite{bertoldo2024aupimo} evaluates per-image overlap over a logarithmic FPR range, providing low-variance measurements at operationally relevant false positive rates. In this work, we report all four ranking metrics alongside threshold-dependent operational metrics (F1, Recall at fixed FPR, Dice) to present a complete picture of detector performance.

\subsection{Threshold Selection and Conformal Prediction}

Deploying an anomaly detector requires converting scores into binary decisions via a threshold. Classic unsupervised approaches include Otsu's method, which maximizes inter-class variance but becomes unstable when normal and anomalous score distributions overlap, and Peaks-Over-Threshold (POT) based on Extreme Value Theory, which models the upper tail of the normal distribution but can be corrupted when the test set contains a non-negligible fraction of anomalies. Conformal prediction~\cite{angelopoulos2023conformal, vovk2005conformal} offers a principled alternative: given $K$ normal calibration samples, the threshold is set at a finite-sample quantile that guarantees FPR~$\leq \alpha$ under the exchangeability assumption, without requiring parametric distributional assumptions. Recent work has advocated risk-based thresholding with FPR guarantees for reliable anomaly detection deployment~\cite{estievenart2025risk}. CalZSAD adopts conformal thresholding and, crucially, precedes it with robust per-class normalization so that the threshold becomes comparable across classes and domains.

% =====================================================================
% III. PROPOSED METHOD
% =====================================================================
\section{Proposed Method}

CalZSAD is a lightweight post-processing module attached to the frozen AA-CLIP backbone. It does not retrain or modify the backbone in any way. The pipeline operates in two phases: a one-time \emph{calibration phase} using $K{=}20$ normal images per class, and an \emph{inference phase} for each test image. Fig.~\ref{fig:pipeline} illustrates the overall pipeline.

\begin{figure*}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{calzsad_architecture.pdf}
\caption{Overview of the CalZSAD pipeline. \textit{Calibration phase (top):} $K$ known-good images of class $c$ pass through the frozen CLIP-ZSAD backbone to produce normal anomaly maps; the top-tail signal module extracts $q = \text{Mean}(\text{Top-}\rho\%(M))$, which is then normalized per class ($z = \mathcal{N}_c(q)$) and used to set the conformal threshold $t_{c,\alpha}$; only the normal label is required. \textit{Inference phase (bottom):} each test image undergoes the same backbone and score processing to produce $z(x)$, which is compared against the stored threshold to yield a binary decision. No backbone retraining is involved.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}

Let $f_\theta$ denote the frozen AA-CLIP model. For an input image $x$, the model produces a pixel-level anomaly map $M(x) \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times W}$ and an image-level anomaly score $s(x) \in \mathbb{R}$. For each target class $c$, we are given a small set of $K$ normal images $X_c^n = \{x_1^n, \ldots, x_K^n\}$ with no defective images and no pixel-level annotations. The goal is to select a threshold $t_{c,\alpha}$ using only $X_c^n$ such that the false positive rate satisfies $\text{FPR}_c(t_{c,\alpha}) \lesssim \alpha$, where the default target is $\alpha = 0.05$.

\subsection{Top-1\% Pixel Signal}

Rather than using the native image-level score $s(x)$ produced by AA-CLIP, CalZSAD derives a more discriminative signal from the pixel-level anomaly map. The pixels of $M(x)$ are sorted in descending order, and the mean of the top 1\% is computed:
\begin{equation}
q(x) = \text{mean}\!\left(\left\{ M(i,j) : M(i,j) \geq Q_{99\%}(M) \right\}\right)
\label{eq:top1pct}
\end{equation}
where $Q_{99\%}(M)$ is the 99th percentile of the anomaly map. This signal captures localized anomalous regions while being more robust than the single maximum pixel, which is susceptible to noise.

\subsection{Robust Per-Class Normalization}

To eliminate score scale shifts across classes, $q(x)$ is standardized per class using robust statistics estimated from the $K$ normal calibration images:
\begin{equation}
z(x) = \frac{q(x) - \mu_c}{\sigma_c}
\label{eq:normalize}
\end{equation}
where $\mu_c = \text{median}(\{q(x_i^n)\}_{i=1}^K)$ is the median of the calibration scores, and $\sigma_c = 1.4826 \times \text{median}(|q_{\text{left}} - \mu_c|)$ is estimated from the Median Absolute Deviation (MAD) computed only on scores below the median. Using only the left half of the distribution prevents anomalous outliers from inflating the dispersion estimate. After normalization, normal images across all classes center around zero with unit spread, making thresholds comparable.

\subsection{Conformal Threshold Selection}

Given the $K$ normalized calibration scores $R_c = \{r_1, \ldots, r_K\}$ sorted in ascending order $r_{(1)} \leq \cdots \leq r_{(K)}$, the conformal threshold for class $c$ at target FPR $\alpha$ is:
\begin{equation}
t_{c,\alpha} = r_{(m)}, \quad m = \min\!\left\{\left\lceil (K+1)(1-\alpha) \right\rceil,\; K\right\}
\label{eq:conformal}
\end{equation}

A test image $x$ is classified as anomalous if $z(x) > t_{c,\alpha}$. Under the exchangeability assumption between calibration and future normal samples, this rule provides a finite-sample guarantee: $\Pr(z(x) > t_{c,\alpha}) \leq \alpha$, without requiring Gaussian or any other parametric assumption about the score distribution~\cite{angelopoulos2023conformal, vovk2005conformal}.

\textbf{Concrete example.} With $K{=}20$ and $\alpha{=}0.05$, we obtain $m = \lceil 21 \times 0.95 \rceil = 20$. The threshold is thus the maximum of the 20 calibration scores, yielding a theoretical FPR of $1/(K{+}1) = 1/21 \approx 4.76\%$.

\textbf{Pixel-level extension.} For pixel-level segmentation, the empirical quantile is computed over all pixels from the $K$ normal images at a target pixel-FAR of $\beta{=}1\%$, and the anomaly map is binarized accordingly: $\hat{Y}_{\text{pixel}}(x) = \mathbb{1}[M(x) \geq \tau_{c,\beta}]$.

% =====================================================================
% IV. EXPERIMENTS
% =====================================================================
\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\textbf{Backbone.} We use AA-CLIP~\cite{ma2025aaclip} as the sole backbone. AA-CLIP is a CVPR~2025 method that separates normal and abnormal semantics in the CLIP language space via anomaly-aware anchors and residual visual adapters. It is trained on the VisA dataset and applied zero-shot to all target domains.

\textbf{Datasets.} We evaluate on six datasets spanning two domains:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Industrial (3 datasets):} MVTec-AD~\cite{bergmann2019mvtec} (15 classes), BTAD~\cite{mishra2021btad} (3 classes), and MPDD~\cite{jezek2021mpdd} (6 classes).
\item \textbf{Medical (3 datasets):} Brain MRI, Liver CT, and Retina OCT, all sourced from the BMAD benchmark~\cite{bao2023bmad}.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Protocol.} All metrics are computed per-class, then macro-averaged across classes within each dataset, and finally macro-averaged across datasets. CalZSAD uses $K{=}20$ normal images per class for calibration, sampled from the official train-normal split when available; for medical datasets without a dedicated train split, $K$ images are randomly sampled from normal test images and excluded from evaluation. Results are averaged over 8 random seeds for image-level and 3 seeds for pixel-level experiments. The image-level FPR target is $\alpha{=}5\%$; the pixel-level FAR target is $\beta{=}1\%$.

\textbf{Metrics.} We report both ranking metrics that do not require a threshold (image-AUROC, image-AP, pixel-AUROC, pixel-AP, AUPRO~\cite{bergmann2020aupro}, and AUPIMO~\cite{bertoldo2024aupimo}) and operational metrics at a fixed operating point: FPR, Recall, F1 (image level), and Dice at FAR~1\% with pixel-FPR (pixel level). We also report the \emph{Oracle~F1}, defined as the best F1 achievable by setting the per-class threshold at exactly FPR~=~5\% using ground-truth labels. This oracle serves as an upper bound that no unsupervised threshold method can exceed.

\subsection{Image-Level Results}

Table~\ref{tab:image_results} presents the image-level results. The ``Ref.'' column shows the AUROC reported in the original AA-CLIP paper~\cite{ma2025aaclip} under their full experimental configuration, while ``Ours'' shows our reproduction of the AA-CLIP model evaluated on the six target datasets. Minor differences between the two columns are expected due to variations in data splits and preprocessing. The remaining columns report operational metrics produced by CalZSAD at the target FPR of 5\%.

