\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{ll cc c ccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Domain} & \textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{img-AUROC} & \textbf{img-AUROC} & \textbf{$\Delta$} & \textbf{img-AP} & \textbf{FPR (\%)} & \textbf{Recall (\%)} & \textbf{F1 (\%)} & \textbf{Oracle F1 (\%)} \\
 &  & \textbf{(Ref.)} & \textbf{(Ours)} & & & & & & \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{Industrial}
 & BTAD     & 94.8 & 93.98 & $-$0.82 & 93.09 & 5.74 & 64.47 & 74.26 & 79.69 \\
 & MPDD     & 75.1 & 73.06 & $-$2.04 & 78.49 & 3.79 & 30.27 & 46.21 & 49.62 \\
 & MVTec-AD & 90.5 & 84.92 & $-$5.58 & 92.94 & 4.41 & 72.74 & 83.70 & 86.47 \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{Medical}
 & Brain    & 80.2 & 58.40 & $-$21.80 & 86.30 & 3.43 & 21.41 & 34.26 & 42.92 \\
 & Liver    & 69.7 & 57.16 & $-$12.54 & 53.74 & 0.94 &  2.16 &  4.09 & 13.55 \\
 & Retina   & 82.7 & 71.49 & $-$11.21 & 65.50 & 5.02 & 51.54 & 63.81 & 71.94 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}%
}
\end{table*}

Several observations emerge from Table~\ref{tab:image_results}. First, our reproduction closely matches the original AA-CLIP numbers on industrial datasets (within 0.8 to 5.6 points of AUROC), while medical datasets show larger gaps (11 to 22 points). This discrepancy is expected because the medical datasets in BMAD have different preprocessing and evaluation splits compared to the setup used in the original AA-CLIP paper, and our reproduction runs the released checkpoint without any modification.

Second, CalZSAD successfully controls FPR near the 5\% target across all six datasets, ranging from 0.94\% (Liver) to 5.74\% (BTAD). This demonstrates that the conformal thresholding mechanism, combined with robust normalization, produces stable operating points regardless of the score distribution of each class.

Third, industrial datasets achieve strong F1 scores: 83.70 on MVTec-AD, 74.26 on BTAD, and 46.21 on MPDD, all within 3 to 6 points of their respective oracle F1 values. For medical datasets, Retina achieves a competitive F1 of 63.81 (oracle: 71.94). Brain and Liver exhibit lower F1 scores (34.26 and 4.09, respectively), but this reflects a fundamental limitation of the AA-CLIP backbone on these domains rather than a failure of the threshold selection. The oracle F1 for Liver is only 13.55, indicating that the backbone itself cannot separate normal from anomalous images at FPR~$\leq$~5\%.

Fourth, image-AP provides a complementary view. MVTec-AD and BTAD have high AP (above~92), confirming strong ranking. Liver's AP of 53.74 is much lower, corroborating the low F1 and oracle values.

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{aaclip_score_shift.png}
\caption{Raw anomaly scores of normal images (AA-CLIP) shift substantially across datasets. Because the normal score distributions do not share the same scale, a single fixed threshold would yield very different FPR values across classes. This motivates the robust per-class normalization step in CalZSAD (Section~III-C).}
\label{fig:score_shift}
\end{figure}

