The pixel-level results reveal several important findings. First, our pixel-AUROC reproduction is very close to the original paper, with differences within $\pm$0.75 points across all datasets, and in three cases (BTAD, MVTec-AD, Retina) our reproduction slightly exceeds the reported values.

Second, the gap between pixel-AUROC and pixel-AP is striking, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:pixel_inflation}. All six datasets have pixel-AUROC above 92\%, yet pixel-AP ranges from 9.96 (Liver) to 63.54 (Retina). This confirms the well-documented AUROC inflation phenomenon under extreme class imbalance~\cite{saito2015pr}: because over 99\% of pixels are normal, even a mediocre anomaly map can achieve high AUROC. The gap of 40 to 88 points between pixel-AUROC and pixel-AP underscores why operational metrics such as Dice at a fixed FAR are necessary for honest evaluation.

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_pixel_inflation.png}
\caption{Pixel-AUROC inflation: comparison with pixel-AP and AUPIMO. The gap of 40 to 88 points between pixel-AUROC and pixel-AP is a consequence of extreme class imbalance at the pixel level (over 99\% of pixels are normal), showing that high pixel-AUROC does not imply accurate localization.}
\label{fig:pixel_inflation}
\end{figure}

Third, AUPRO and AUPIMO provide complementary rankings. AUPIMO, which evaluates per-image overlap at low FPR~\cite{bertoldo2024aupimo}, reveals that MVTec-AD (78.12) and BTAD (75.95) have the best localization quality, while Brain (35.46) and MPDD (42.25) lag behind despite having high pixel-AUROC.

Fourth, the Dice@FAR1\% scores show that CalZSAD achieves reasonable segmentation with pixel-FPR tightly controlled around 1\% (range: 0.87 to 1.09\%). Retina achieves the highest Dice (50.11), while MPDD has the lowest (22.15). These values are consistent with the ranking suggested by AUPIMO and pixel-AP, rather than the misleadingly uniform pixel-AUROC.

\subsection{Analysis and Discussion}

\textbf{Gap to oracle.} At the image level, CalZSAD's F1 is within 5 to 9 points of the oracle F1 for industrial datasets, demonstrating that the conformal threshold closely approximates the best possible threshold at the target FPR. For medical datasets, the absolute gap is larger (8 to 9 points), but this is proportional to the lower oracle values themselves.

\textbf{Domain gap.} The most notable performance disparity is between industrial and medical domains. On industrial datasets, AA-CLIP achieves img-AUROC of 73 to 94 with strong operational metrics. On medical datasets, img-AUROC drops to 57 to 71, and F1 at FPR~5\% is substantially lower (4 to 64). This gap is not caused by threshold selection but by the backbone's limited ability to generalize to medical imaging modalities that differ substantially from the natural images used to train CLIP. The Liver dataset is particularly challenging, with oracle F1 of only 13.55, indicating that the backbone barely separates normal from anomalous liver CT images at low FPR.

\textbf{Pixel-AUROC inflation.} Across all six datasets, pixel-AUROC exceeds 92\% while pixel-AP ranges from 10\% to 64\%. This 30 to 80 point gap is a direct consequence of the extreme class imbalance at the pixel level and highlights the importance of reporting complementary metrics. Had we relied solely on pixel-AUROC, the Liver dataset (px-AUROC 97.71, px-AP 9.96) would appear to have excellent segmentation when in fact the anomaly map is highly imprecise for the positive class. This finding aligns with prior work advocating for precision-recall-based evaluation~\cite{saito2015pr} and low-FPR metrics~\cite{bertoldo2024aupimo}.

\textbf{Practical deployment.} CalZSAD requires only 20 normal images per class, which are readily available in any production setting or clinical database, and runs entirely on CPU in seconds since it only computes order statistics on precomputed scores. The conformal guarantee ensures that FPR stays near the target without tuning, making the system suitable for deployment where false alarm budgets are strict.

% =====================================================================
% V. CONCLUSION
% =====================================================================
\section{Conclusion}

This work addresses the gap between benchmark AUROC and practical deployability of CLIP-based zero-shot anomaly detection. Using AA-CLIP as the backbone, we apply CalZSAD, a post-processing framework combining robust per-class score normalization, a top-1\% pixel signal for improved image-level discrimination, and conformal prediction-based threshold selection with only 20 normal images per class. Experiments on six datasets covering industrial (BTAD, MPDD, MVTec-AD) and medical (Brain, Liver, Retina) domains show that CalZSAD controls FPR at the target level of approximately 5\% while achieving F1 within 5 to 9 points of the oracle upper bound. At the pixel level, Dice at FAR~1\% closely tracks the oracle with pixel-FPR tightly around 1\%.

Our comprehensive evaluation also reveals important findings for the community. First, pixel-AUROC is heavily inflated (30 to 80 points above pixel-AP) due to extreme class imbalance, making it an unreliable standalone metric. Second, medical datasets pose a significantly greater challenge than industrial ones, with the gap attributable to the backbone's limited transferability to medical modalities rather than threshold selection. Third, threshold selection methods should always be evaluated alongside their induced FPR, as high F1 values in the literature often correspond to uncontrolled FPR levels of 10 to 20\%.

\textbf{Limitations.} Liver and Brain datasets yield low F1 even with oracle thresholds, indicating a fundamental backbone limitation on medical domains. Improving this requires better CLIP-ZSAD models for medical imaging, which is beyond the scope of threshold calibration. Furthermore, the current evaluation uses $K{=}20$; reducing $K$ below 10 increases FPR variance and requires further study.

\textbf{Future work.} Extending CalZSAD to newer backbones and harder benchmarks (MVTec-AD~2, Real-IAD), reducing the calibration set size via stronger estimators, and combining CalZSAD with domain adaptation for medical images are promising directions.

